1. Scope and Methodological Boundary

This chapter addresses a structural gap in the MODV research program. Thesis I established a carrier-level optimization framework determining the optimal degree of modularity N^* and population vector \mathbf{N}^* for a Starship-class deployment vehicle. In that formulation, modules were treated as parameterized black boxes characterized by mass, volume, function, and interface type. The internal architecture of those modules was assumed but not derived.

The present chapter removes that abstraction. We define the internal design space of Orbital Modular Units (OMUs) and formalize the functional taxonomy that Thesis I presupposed. The central questions are twofold. First, given a functional requirement—habitat, laboratory, structure, power, thermal, robotic assembly, command, propulsion, or life support—what is the optimal internal subsystem architecture of the module satisfying that requirement? Second, to what degree should that architecture be shared across module classes rather than custom-built for each one?

These questions are not restatements of Thesis I's optimization. The carrier-level utility function U(\mathcal{A}) treats module mass m_i as an input. This chapter treats m_i as an output of a lower-level optimization over subsystem allocation. The two optimizations are coupled: module-level design choices change the m_i and m_{I,i} values feeding Thesis I's constraints. Section 9 formalizes this coupling as a fixed-point iteration and proves convergence under Lipschitz conditions.

A methodological caveat is essential. This chapter presents no new physical experiments, flight tests, or trained machine-learning models. All subsystem masses, power draws, learning rates, reliability rates, and standardization ceilings derive from published manufacturer datasheets, peer-reviewed cost and reliability models, and publicly available flight telemetry. Where quantitative values appear, their sources and uncertainty ranges are stated in the text. Monte Carlo simulations in Section 10 use analytic random sampling from these published distributions; they are computational sensitivity analyses, not empirical validations. The term "empirical" throughout refers to literature-derived data, never to original experimental work.

Recent developments materially affect the analysis. OSAM-1 was cancelled by NASA in February 2024 due to significant technical, cost, and schedule challenges, and low return on investment to the on-orbit servicing community (NASA OIG, 2024). This cancellation is evidence that autonomous assembly without a standardized interface ecosystem is high-risk. Conversely, the iBOSS intelligent Space System Interface (iSSI) achieved in-orbit demonstration on the ISS in 2022 at TRL 7 and was flown on Sidus Space's LizzieSat-3 in March 2025 as a post-launch connectable interface, with NASA ARMADAS and commercial programs now baselining it (iBOSS Consortium, 2025). These developments strengthen the empirical grounding of our interface mass assumptions and motivate the standardization formalism developed here.

