2. Functional Taxonomy and Boundary Definitions

2.1 Ten-Class Taxonomy

We adopt a ten-class functional taxonomy, incorporating resolutions from earlier drafts. Each functional class is a label denoting the primary mission function a module provides. A module may provide a secondary function at reduced capacity, but exactly one primary class is assigned per module for taxonomic purposes.

The ten classes are:

Class Symbol Role Size Class
Habitat H Pressurized crew living volume Core (4–8 m)
Laboratory L Pressurized research/payload volume Core (4–8 m)
Command/Comm C Avionics, GN&C, comms relay Core/Peripheral
Structural S Truss, connective, load-bearing nodes Peripheral (0.4–0.8 m)
Power/Energy E Solar arrays, batteries, PMAD Peripheral (0.4–0.8 m)
Thermal T Radiators, heat pipes, loop control Peripheral (0.4–0.8 m)
Robotic R Manipulator arms, docking assist Peripheral (0.4–0.8 m)
Propulsion P Tug \Delta v, station-keeping, phasing Peripheral (0.4–0.8 m)
ECLSS Core A_c Crew-critical life support Core (4–8 m)
ECLSS Peripheral A_p Regenerative food production, water storage/shielding Peripheral (0.4–0.8 m)

The Propulsion class is separated from Robotic because tug and rendezvous propulsion has a materially different subsystem profile—propellant storage, thrust vector control, and specific impulse trade-offs—than manipulation hardware such as arms and end effectors, with different standardization ceilings and mass fractions. The ECLSS class is split because crew-critical life support (A_c) has fundamentally different reliability, redundancy, and habitability requirements than regenerative peripheral agriculture (A_p), which primarily serves as distributed shielding mass and food supplementation.

2.2 Core–Peripheral Boundary

The core–peripheral boundary is load-bearing for the subsequent analysis. We define a subsystem as core-critical if its failure or non-availability directly violates a NASA-STD-3001 habitability requirement or prevents station attitude or orbit control. All other subsystems are peripheral.

The Command/Comm class (C) is classified as Core when it carries primary station GN&C, timekeeping, and fault-detection avionics; it is Peripheral when it is a standalone communications relay or redundant avionics node relying on station-supplied attitude data. For all subsequent calculations, the nominal architecture assumes C is Core.

2.3 A_c Sub-Tagging

ISS operational precedent separates Water Recovery System (WRS), Oxygen Generation Assembly (OGA), and Carbon Dioxide Removal Assembly (CDRA) as distinct rack-level architectures. To reflect this, we maintain A_c as a single top-level class for carrier-level modularity calculations while internally sub-tagging the subsystem allocation vector with A_{c,\text{WRS}}, A_{c,\text{OGA}}, and A_{c,\text{CDRA}}. This prevents artificial inflation of carrier interface complexity while preserving subsystem-level granularity for standardization and learning-curve calculations.

The boundary between A_c and A_p is defined by NASA-STD-3001 required life-support functions. A_c includes atmosphere revitalization, potable water processing, and fire detection and suppression. A_p includes supplemental food production, non-potable water storage, and radiation-shielding water.

2.4 Coverage Claim

We do not claim that the ten-class taxonomy is formally complete for all conceivable future orbital architectures. Rather, we claim functional coverage under the stated architectural boundary conditions: a core–periphery station with pressurized crew volume, robotic assembly, and standardized interfaces, deployed by a Starship-class carrier.

Let \mathcal{R}_{\text{NASA}} denote the set of functional requirements derived from NASA-STD-3001 Volume 2 and typical station operations concepts. Let \mathcal{F} denote the ten-class functional set. Then we assert:

\forall r \in \mathcal{R}_{\text{NASA}}, \quad \exists f_i \in \mathcal{F} : r \mapsto f_i.

Each NASA-STD-3001 requirement maps to at least one class: atmosphere revitalization maps to A_c (OGA and CDRA sub-tags); temperature and humidity control maps to A_c and T; fire detection and suppression maps to A_c and S; food and water map to A_c (potable) and A_p (non-potable); waste management maps to A_c; crew health and habitability map to H; command and control map to C; power generation and storage map to E; structural integrity maps to S; docking and berthing map to C, R, and S; station-keeping and attitude control map to P and C. No requirement requires an eleventh class under the stated boundary conditions.

The Vast Haven-1 rotational gravity concept is accommodated within H, S, and C via vehicle-level rotation, not a dedicated functional class.

