3. Universal Subsystem Ontology

3.1 Subsystem Catalog

Let \mathcal{U} = \{u_1, u_2, \ldots, u_p\} denote the universal catalog of subsystems available to any module. We adopt p = 14: structure, primary power generation, energy storage, power distribution and management (PMAD), avionics/flight computer, attitude determination and control (ADCS), thermal control, communications, docking/berthing interface hardware, robotic manipulation hardware, propulsion hardware, ECLSS components, payload racks, and radiation shielding.

Each subsystem u_j is characterized by:

· Capability metric q_j with defined units (W for power, m^3/day for water processing, N·m·s for ADCS momentum capacity, etc.);
· Mass m_j, power draw P_j, volume V_j;
· Technology readiness level TRL_j;
· First-unit cost c_{1,j} and learning rate LR_j;
· Non-recurring engineering cost for catalog qualification \text{NRE}_j;
· Interface requirements to the module bus.

3.2 Capability Fraction

For module class i and subsystem u_j, we define the capability fraction:

a_{i,j} = \frac{q_{i,j}}{q_{j,\max}} \in [0,1],

where q_{i,j} is the capability of subsystem u_j instantiated in class i, and q_{j,\max} is the maximum required capability for that subsystem across all module classes in the fleet. For binary subsystems such as a docking interface, q is 1 if present and 0 if absent.

This operationalization makes the standardization index a capability-weighted measure rather than a count of shared parts. It is a deliberate choice: a subsystem providing 10 kW of power contributes more to the commonality measure than a binary docking interface, because its mass, cost, and learning-curve impact are larger.

