4. Module Design Space Formalization

4.1 Subsystem Allocation Vector

For a module of class i, the subsystem allocation vector is:

\mathbf{a}_i = (a_{i,1}, a_{i,2}, \ldots, a_{i,p}), \quad a_{i,j} \in [0,1].

The total subsystem capability weight of class i is:

w_i = \sum_{j=1}^{p} a_{i,j}.

4.2 Standardization Index

For subsystem u_j as instantiated in module class i, let \mathbf{c}_{i,j} denote the specific component design. Let \mathcal{C}_{\text{shared}} denote the set of catalog-shared components. The standardization index of module class i is:

\sigma_i = \frac{\sum_{j=1}^{p} a_{i,j} \cdot \mathbb{1}[\mathbf{c}_{i,j} \in \mathcal{C}_{\text{shared}}]}{\sum_{j=1}^{p} a_{i,j}}.

For parametric design families, we extend the indicator. Two component designs belong to the same parametric family \mathcal{F}_j if and only if:

1. They share the same subsystem architecture code (e.g., deployable rigid-panel solar array, cold-gas thruster, reaction wheel assembly).
2. All key performance parameters lie within \pm 50\% of the family baseline.
3. Mechanical and electrical interfaces to the module bus are identical.
4. Qualification status and dominant failure modes are unchanged.

Then:

\delta_{\text{param}}(\mathbf{c}_{i,j}, \mathbf{c}_{i',j}) =
\begin{cases}
1 & \text{if both belong to the same parametric family } \mathcal{F}_j,\\
0 & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

This criterion prevents the parametric extension from inflating standardization by grouping substantively different designs.

4.3 Capability-Weighted Degree of Commonality Index

For a fleet of module classes with population vector \mathbf{N} = (N_1, \ldots, N_k), the Capability-Weighted Degree of Commonality Index (CW-DCI) is:

\text{CW-DCI} = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{k} N_i \sum_{j=1}^{p} a_{i,j} \cdot \mathbb{1}[\mathbf{c}_{i,j} \in \mathcal{C}_{\text{shared}}]}{\sum_{i=1}^{k} N_i \sum_{j=1}^{p} a_{i,j}}.

This is a capability-weighted average of the per-class standardization indices. We use the term Capability-Weighted to signal that subsystems with larger capability fractions contribute proportionally more to the index.

4.4 Commonality Bound

Theorem 4.1. For a fixed taxonomy with core classes having population-weighted capability w_i, and given maximum achievable subsystem-resolved standardization \sigma_i^{\max} for each class, the fleet CW-DCI is bounded above by:

\text{CW-DCI} \le 1 - \frac{\sum_{i \in \text{core}} N_i w_i (1 - \sigma_i^{\max})}{\sum_{i=1}^{k} N_i w_i}.

Proof. By definition, a core class i can contribute at most \sigma_i^{\max} w_i to the numerator of CW-DCI; peripheral classes can contribute at most w_i. Thus:

\text{CW-DCI} \le \frac{\sum_{i \in \text{core}} N_i \sigma_i^{\max} w_i + \sum_{i \in \text{periph}} N_i w_i}{\sum_{i=1}^{k} N_i w_i}.

Subtracting from 1 yields the stated bound. \square

Corollary 4.1. With \sigma_{\text{core}}^{\max} \approx 0.15–0.35 and \sigma_{\text{periph}}^{\max} \approx 0.85–0.95, at Thesis I's core–periphery ratio of 1:20–1:25, the maximum CW-DCI is approximately 0.72–0.81. At NASA CLD ratios of 1:10–1:15, it falls to 0.54–0.63. This quantifies the trade-off between population scale and fleet-level commonality.

