5. Standardization Economics and Learning Curves

5.1 Wright's Law Cost Model

We adopt Wright's law: the cost of the n-th unit of a component produced in a shared catalog is:

c(n) = c_1 \cdot n^{\log_2(LR)},

where c_1 is the first-unit cost and LR \in (0,1) is the learning rate. Subsystem-dependent learning rates from SSCM19 and OneWeb/Starlink production data are:

Subsystem LR range
Avionics/flight computer 0.80–0.85
Power (PMAD, batteries) 0.82–0.88
Propulsion (EP, cold gas) 0.85–0.90
Structures (truss, panels) 0.92–0.97
Pressure shells (core) 0.95–0.98

These ranges are used in the Monte Carlo sensitivity analysis of Section 10.

5.2 Production Cost with Fleet-Level NRE

For module class i produced N_i times, with standardization index \sigma_i, the production cost is:

C_i(N_i, \sigma_i) = \sigma_i \sum_{n=1}^{N_i} c_{1,\text{shared}} n^{\log_2 LR} + (1-\sigma_i) N_i c_{\text{custom}}.

Catalog NRE is a fleet-level fixed cost, not a per-class variable cost:

C_{\text{catalog}} = \sum_{j \in \mathcal{C}_{\text{shared}}} \text{NRE}_j.

Total fleet production cost is:

C_{\text{total}} = \sum_{i=1}^{k} C_i(N_i, \sigma_i) + C_{\text{catalog}}.

5.3 Boundary Optimum Theorem

Theorem 5.1 (Standardization Boundary Optimum). If c_{\text{custom}} > c_{1,\text{shared}} and N_i is sufficiently large such that:

\sum_{n=1}^{N_i} c_{1,\text{shared}} n^{\log_2 LR} < N_i c_{\text{custom}},

then the cost-minimizing standardization level for module class i, ignoring operational penalties, is \sigma_i^* = \sigma_i^{\max}.

Proof. The derivative of C_i with respect to \sigma_i is:

\frac{\partial C_i}{\partial \sigma_i} = \sum_{n=1}^{N_i} c_{1,\text{shared}} n^{\log_2 LR} - N_i c_{\text{custom}}.

By the condition, this derivative is negative for all \sigma_i \in [0,1]. Hence C_i is monotonically decreasing in \sigma_i, and the minimum occurs at the upper boundary \sigma_i = \sigma_i^{\max}. \square

Corollary 5.1. Unlike carrier-level modularity, which has an interior optimum from integration complexity, module-level standardization has no interior trade-off when NRE is fleet-level: push \sigma_i to its functional ceiling.

5.4 Catalog Inclusion Criterion

Theorem 5.2. Subsystem u_j should be included in the shared catalog if and only if:

\sum_{i=1}^{k} N_i \left[ c_{\text{custom},j} - \frac{1}{N_i}\sum_{n=1}^{N_i} c_{1,\text{shared},j} n^{\log_2 LR_j} \right] > \text{NRE}_j.

Equivalently, the fleet-wide learning-curve savings must exceed the one-time NRE cost.

Proof. Inclusion of u_j changes fleet cost by:

\Delta = -\sum_i N_i c_{\text{custom},j} + \sum_i \sum_{n=1}^{N_i} c_{1,\text{shared},j} n^{\log_2 LR_j} + \text{NRE}_j.

Setting \Delta < 0 gives the criterion. \square

5.5 Interior Optimum Under Operational Penalty

Let P_i(\sigma_i) be an operational mass penalty function representing performance degradation due to forced subsystem standardization. Examples include non-optimal radiator area causing thermal inefficiency, or off-nominal thruster specific impulse causing increased propellant consumption. Let total lifecycle cost be:

J_i(N_i, \sigma_i) = C_i(N_i, \sigma_i) + \lambda N_i P_i(\sigma_i),

where \lambda > 0 is the cost per unit mass of operational penalty.

Define:

A_i = \sum_{n=1}^{N_i} c_{1,\text{shared}} n^{\log_2 LR} - N_i c_{\text{custom}}.

The first derivative of J_i is:

J_i'(\sigma_i) = A_i + \lambda N_i P_i'(\sigma_i).

Theorem 5.3 (Three-Regime Standardization Optimum). Assume P_i is twice continuously differentiable with P_i''(\sigma_i) > 0 for all \sigma_i \in [0, \sigma_i^{\max}], and P_i'(0) = 0. Then:

\sigma_i^* =
\begin{cases}
0, & \text{if } J_i'(0) \ge 0,\\[4pt]
(0, \sigma_i^{\max}), & \text{if } J_i'(0) < 0 < J_i'(\sigma_i^{\max}),\\[4pt]
\sigma_i^{\max}, & \text{if } J_i'(\sigma_i^{\max}) \le 0.
\end{cases}

Proof. Since P_i'' > 0, the function J_i'(\sigma_i) is strictly increasing on [0, \sigma_i^{\max}]. The three regimes correspond to the sign of J_i' at the endpoints.

In the first regime, J_i'(0) \ge 0, so J_i'(\sigma_i) > 0 for all \sigma_i > 0, implying J_i is increasing and the minimum is at \sigma_i = 0.

In the second regime, J_i'(0) < 0 and J_i'(\sigma_i^{\max}) > 0. By the Intermediate Value Theorem, there exists a unique \sigma_i^* \in (0, \sigma_i^{\max}) with J_i'(\sigma_i^*) = 0. Since J_i'' = \lambda N_i P_i'' > 0, this is a strict local minimum.

In the third regime, J_i'(\sigma_i^{\max}) \le 0, so J_i'(\sigma_i) < 0 for all \sigma_i < \sigma_i^{\max}, implying J_i is decreasing and the minimum is at \sigma_i = \sigma_i^{\max}. \square

This theorem is stronger than a simple interior-optimum existence result. It provides a complete trichotomy: boundary at zero, interior optimum, or boundary at the functional ceiling.

The convexity assumption on P_i requires justification. For thermal radiators, the penalty of forcing a standardized area into a non-optimal view-factor position is a smooth function of the geometric mismatch, reasonably approximated as convex. For solar arrays, the penalty of non-optimal sizing is also smooth. For discrete subsystems such as reaction wheels with fixed momentum capacity steps, the penalty is piecewise constant, and the theorem requires modification. We note this limitation explicitly.

