6. Internal Interconnect Mass Model

6.1 Definition

Thesis I modeled module-to-module interface mass but neglected subsystem-to-subsystem interconnects inside each module. These include power bus taps, avionics data bus drops, thermal loop branches, structural mounts, and harnessing. For module class i:

m_{\text{internal},i} = \sum_{j=1}^{p} a_{i,j} \mu_j,

where \mu_j is the per-unit-capability interconnect mass for subsystem u_j.

6.2 Derivation from Published SmallSat Bus Data

We derive \mu_j values from published dry masses and subsystem counts for Pumpkin MISC-2, Blue Canyon XB1, GomSpace NanoPower, and AAC Clyde Space Starbuck platforms. Harness and connector mass fractions typically range from 3% to 8% of dry mass for well-integrated bus architectures with star topology on power and data buses. For first-generation ad hoc designs, pairwise interconnect complexity can raise this to 10–15%.

For peripheral modules with dry mass 35–90 kg, we adopt:

m_{\text{internal},i} = (0.05 \pm 0.02) \cdot m_{\text{dry},i},

where the uncertainty is one standard deviation. For core modules, the fraction is lower (3–6%) due to larger subsystem integration efficiency, but absolute mass is higher.

This model assumes bus topology for power and data with no pairwise custom wiring, and constant \mu_j within a class. The length-dependent harness term is folded into the dry mass fraction. Values are derived from published smallsat buses, not validated by physical tests in this work.

6.3 Corrected Interface Mass Fractions

For a 40 kg S-class module with iSSI interface (0.9 kg):

· Previous (Thesis I): f_{\text{interface}} = 0.9/40 = 2.3\%.
· Corrected (including internal interconnect mass 5% of dry = 2.0 kg): f_{\text{interface+internal}} = (0.9+2.0)/40 = 7.3\%.

For a core H-class module (15,000 kg) with IDSS interface (300 kg) and internal interconnects (3–6% = 450–900 kg):

· Previous: 2.0%.
· Corrected: 5.0–8.0%.

This correction is applied to all mass tuples in Section 8.

