7. Consolidation and Fault-Tree Analysis

7.1 Phase-Dependent Fault-Tree Methodology

We use fault-tree analysis (FTA) with generic component reliability data from MIL-HDBK-217F and NPRD-2016 at 95% confidence. No physical testing was performed. For a consolidated module, we construct fault trees for each undesired top event, such as loss of propulsion and robotics simultaneously. Failure probabilities P(e) are computed from Boolean logic of basic events with exponential failure models over a five-year mission.

We introduce phase-dependent criticality weights \kappa_t:

R_{\text{consolidated}} = \sum_{e \in \text{Faults}} P(e) \cdot \left[ \kappa_{\text{asm}} I_{\text{asm}}(e) + \kappa_{\text{ops}} I_{\text{ops}}(e) \right],

where \kappa_{\text{asm}} = 0.75 for the assembly phase and \kappa_{\text{ops}} = 0.25 for steady-state operations. I_{\text{asm}}(e) and I_{\text{ops}}(e) are impact factors on a 0–1 ordinal scale, assessed from mission timeline analysis.

The fault isolation penalty reported below is the ratio R_{\text{consolidated}} / R_{\text{baseline}}, where R_{\text{baseline}} is computed for separate modules with no shared fault paths.

7.2 Structural–Robotic Consolidation

The S–R consolidated module combines a structural node with a robotic arm, sharing PMAD, chassis, and avionics. Fault-tree analysis identifies three additional failure modes:

1. Common-cause power failure: shared PMAD disables both structural integrity monitoring and manipulation capability.
2. Structural loading fault propagation: arm operations induce vibrations transmitted through shared structure.
3. Thermal distortion coupling: arm motor heat dissipation causes thermal distortion of the structural node.

Results:

Metric S+R Separate S–R Consolidated \Delta
Total module count 200 168 −16%
Integration complexity 1.00 0.81 −19%
Per-module engineering cost 1.00 1.12 +12%
Fault isolation penalty 1.00 1.14 +14%
Net utility change — — +7%

The consolidation remains net-favorable, but the margin is narrower than earlier estimates. A consolidation ratio exceeding 25% would likely reverse this conclusion.

7.3 Propulsion–Robotic Consolidation

The P–R consolidated module combines propulsion and robotic arm, sharing PMAD, structural chassis, and avionics. Fault-tree analysis identifies:

1. Common-cause avionics failure disabling both propulsion and arm control.
2. Propulsion plume impingement degrading arm joint lubrication and thermal surfaces.
3. Structural coupling: thruster-induced vibrations reduce arm positioning accuracy below required tolerance.

Results:

Metric P+R Separate P–R Consolidated \Delta
Module population 40 units 24 units −40%
Integration complexity Baseline Reduced −22%
Phase-weighted risk 1.00 1.28 +28%

Decision. P–R consolidation is not recommended at current risk levels. The +28% risk penalty outweighs the −22% integration complexity benefit because propulsion and robotics have high assembly-phase criticality and shared failure paths. A fault-tolerant P–R module with redundant avionics and isolated mounts could reduce risk to +10–15%, but adds mass and cost. This is deferred to Chapter 11.

