8.2 Fixed-Point Iteration

The two-layer coupling between Thesis I and this chapter is formalized via an operator \mathcal{T}:

\mathbf{m}^{(k+1)} = \mathcal{T}(\mathbf{m}^{(k)}) = \mathbf{m}_{\text{dry,base}} + \mathbf{m}_{\text{interface}}(N^*(\mathbf{m}^{(k)})) + \mathbf{m}_{\text{internal}}(\boldsymbol{\sigma}^*(\mathbf{m}^{(k)})).

Here N^*(\mathbf{m}) is the optimal module count from Thesis I given mass vector \mathbf{m}, and \boldsymbol{\sigma}^*(\mathbf{m}) is the optimal standardization vector from this chapter given the same masses.

A complication is that N^* is discrete. The operator \mathcal{T} is therefore not differentiable in the classical sense across active-set boundaries where the selected module classes, catalog membership, or door mechanism change.

We resolve this by introducing a local Lipschitz condition rather than a global contraction claim.

Proposition 8.1 (Local Fixed-Point Contraction). Within each locally fixed active-set region—where the selected module classes, catalog membership, and door mechanism remain unchanged—the operator \mathcal{T} is Lipschitz with estimated local contraction constant L \le 0.34 < 1.

Under this condition, the fixed-point iteration converges to a unique fixed point within the active-set region:

\mathbf{m}^{(k)} \to \mathbf{m}^*, \quad \mathcal{T}(\mathbf{m}^*) = \mathbf{m}^*.

Transitions between active-set regions are handled separately by checking whether the fixed point remains interior to the region. If it exits the region, the active set is updated and iteration resumes.

The numerical Lipschitz estimate L \le 0.34 is computed by finite-difference gradient estimation of \mathcal{T} at the converged solution, with step sizes chosen to remain within the active-set region. It is a local estimate, not a global bound.

