9. Two-Level Modularity Principle

The central structural result of this chapter is a distinction between two optimization regimes. At the carrier level (Thesis I), modularity has an interior optimum because additional modules increase integration, deployment, and interface complexity. At the module level (this chapter), standardization tends toward its functional ceiling when shared-catalog NRE is amortized fleet-wide and no operational penalty is imposed.

We formalize this as the Two-Level Modularity Principle:

At the carrier level, the optimal number of modules N^* is generally interior-optimal, balancing the benefits of modular decomposition against integration complexity. At the module level, the optimal standardization index \sigma_i^* is boundary-optimal at \sigma_i^{\max} when fleet-level NRE dominates, and interior-optimal only when operational penalties from standardization are sufficiently convex.

This principle is not a theorem but a structural observation about the mathematical form of the two optimization problems. It organizes the theoretical contributions of this chapter and provides a framework for subsequent analysis.

