10. Monte Carlo Uncertainty Propagation

10.1 Parameter Distributions

Maximum standardization ceilings are modeled as Beta-distributed random variables:

\sigma_{\text{peripheral}}^{\max} \sim \text{Beta}(12, 2),

with mean \mu = 0.857 and standard deviation \sigma = 0.089.

\sigma_{\text{core}}^{\max} \sim \text{Beta}(3, 9),

with mean \mu = 0.250 and standard deviation \sigma = 0.120.

Learning rates are sampled uniformly within their published ranges from Section 5.1. Internal interconnect mass fractions are sampled from \mathcal{N}(0.05, 0.02^2) truncated to [0.03, 0.08] for peripheral modules, and \mathcal{N}(0.045, 0.015^2) for core modules.

10.2 Monte Carlo Method

We perform N_{\text{sim}} = 10{,}000 Monte Carlo iterations. Each iteration samples the parameter distributions, recomputes the fixed-point iteration with a maximum of five iterations, and records N^* and CW-DCI. This is a purely computational sensitivity analysis; no machine-learning training or physical experiments are involved.

