13. Conclusion

This chapter has moved the MODV research program one level down from carrier-level module count to module-internal architecture. The central result is the Two-Level Modularity Principle: carrier-level modularity is generally interior-optimal, while module-level standardization tends toward its functional ceiling when shared-catalog NRE is amortized fleet-wide.

The mathematical spine of the chapter consists of four results. Proposition 1 (Coverage) asserts that the ten-class taxonomy covers all NASA-STD-3001 requirements under the stated boundary conditions. Theorem 5.1 (Standardization Boundary Optimum) proves that module-level standardization has no interior trade-off when NRE is fleet-level. Theorem 5.3 (Three-Regime Standardization Optimum) provides the complete trichotomy including the interior-optimum case under convex operational penalties. Proposition 8.1 (Local Fixed-Point Contraction) establishes convergence of the two-layer coupling within active-set regions.

The empirical grounding derives from published flight data, manufacturer datasheets, and peer-reviewed cost models. The iSSI flight heritage, the OSAM-1 cancellation, and OTV operational telemetry provide contemporary evidence for the standardization argument. No physical experiments or trained machine-learning models were used; all results are analytic estimates with explicitly propagated uncertainties.

The corrected mass vector shifts Thesis I's optimal modularity from N^* = 188 to N^* = 170 \pm 8, a nominal reduction of 9.6%. The Monte Carlo analysis confirms that peripheral classes can achieve standardization ceilings above 0.75 with parametric extension, while core classes remain bounded below 0.35. The structural–robotic consolidation remains net-favorable at +7% utility, while the propulsion–robotic consolidation is not recommended at +28% risk penalty.

