Appendix B: Proofs of Theorems

B.1 Theorem 4.1: Commonality Bound

Statement. For a fixed taxonomy with core classes having population-weighted capability w_i, and given maximum achievable subsystem-resolved standardization \sigma_i^{\max} for each class, the fleet CW-DCI is bounded above by:

\text{CW-DCI} \le 1 - \frac{\sum_{i \in \text{core}} N_i w_i (1 - \sigma_i^{\max})}{\sum_{i=1}^{k} N_i w_i}.

Proof.

The CW-DCI is defined as:

\text{CW-DCI} = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{k} N_i \sum_{j=1}^{p} a_{i,j} \cdot \mathbb{1}[\mathbf{c}_{i,j} \in \mathcal{C}_{\text{shared}}]}{\sum_{i=1}^{k} N_i \sum_{j=1}^{p} a_{i,j}}.

For any class i, the numerator contribution is bounded by \sigma_i^{\max} w_i, where w_i = \sum_j a_{i,j}. This follows from the definition of \sigma_i^{\max} as the maximum achievable standardization for that class.

For peripheral classes, \sigma_i^{\max} \le 1, so their contribution is bounded by w_i. For core classes, \sigma_i^{\max} < 1, so their contribution is bounded by \sigma_i^{\max} w_i.

Therefore:

\text{CW-DCI} \le \frac{\sum_{i \in \text{core}} N_i \sigma_i^{\max} w_i + \sum_{i \in \text{periph}} N_i w_i}{\sum_{i=1}^{k} N_i w_i}.

Subtracting from 1:

1 - \text{CW-DCI} \ge 1 - \frac{\sum_{i \in \text{core}} N_i \sigma_i^{\max} w_i + \sum_{i \in \text{periph}} N_i w_i}{\sum_{i=1}^{k} N_i w_i}.

= \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{k} N_i w_i - \sum_{i \in \text{core}} N_i \sigma_i^{\max} w_i - \sum_{i \in \text{periph}} N_i w_i}{\sum_{i=1}^{k} N_i w_i}.

= \frac{\sum_{i \in \text{core}} N_i w_i - \sum_{i \in \text{core}} N_i \sigma_i^{\max} w_i}{\sum_{i=1}^{k} N_i w_i}.

= \frac{\sum_{i \in \text{core}} N_i w_i (1 - \sigma_i^{\max})}{\sum_{i=1}^{k} N_i w_i}.

Therefore:

\text{CW-DCI} \le 1 - \frac{\sum_{i \in \text{core}} N_i w_i (1 - \sigma_i^{\max})}{\sum_{i=1}^{k} N_i w_i}.

\square

B.2 Theorem 5.1: Standardization Boundary Optimum

Statement. If c_{\text{custom}} > c_{1,\text{shared}} and N_i is sufficiently large such that:

\sum_{n=1}^{N_i} c_{1,\text{shared}} n^{\log_2 LR} < N_i c_{\text{custom}},

then the cost-minimizing standardization level for module class i, ignoring operational penalties, is \sigma_i^* = \sigma_i^{\max}.

Proof.

The production cost for module class i is:

C_i(N_i, \sigma_i) = \sigma_i \sum_{n=1}^{N_i} c_{1,\text{shared}} n^{\log_2 LR} + (1-\sigma_i) N_i c_{\text{custom}}.

The derivative with respect to \sigma_i is:

\frac{\partial C_i}{\partial \sigma_i} = \sum_{n=1}^{N_i} c_{1,\text{shared}} n^{\log_2 LR} - N_i c_{\text{custom}}.

Let:

S = \sum_{n=1}^{N_i} c_{1,\text{shared}} n^{\log_2 LR}.

Since LR \in (0,1), we have \log_2 LR < 0, so n^{\log_2 LR} is a decreasing function of n. The sum S grows sublinearly with N_i.

The condition S < N_i c_{\text{custom}} implies:

\frac{\partial C_i}{\partial \sigma_i} < 0 \quad \forall \sigma_i \in [0,1].

Therefore C_i is strictly decreasing in \sigma_i, and the minimum occurs at the upper boundary \sigma_i = 1. However, the functional constraint \sigma_i \le \sigma_i^{\max} restricts the feasible region to [0, \sigma_i^{\max}]. Since C_i is decreasing on this interval, the minimum occurs at \sigma_i = \sigma_i^{\max}.

\square

Remark on the condition. The condition S < N_i c_{\text{custom}} is satisfied when the learning-curve savings per unit exceed the cost difference. For large N_i, the average cost under learning is:

\frac{S}{N_i} = \frac{c_{1,\text{shared}}}{N_i} \sum_{n=1}^{N_i} n^{\log_2 LR} \approx \frac{c_{1,\text{shared}}}{1 + \log_2 LR} N_i^{\log_2 LR}.

This grows sublinearly, so for sufficiently large N_i, the average shared cost becomes much smaller than c_{\text{custom}}.

B.3 Theorem 5.2: Catalog Inclusion Criterion

Statement. Subsystem u_j should be included in the shared catalog if and only if:

\sum_{i=1}^{k} N_i \left[ c_{\text{custom},j} - \frac{1}{N_i}\sum_{n=1}^{N_i} c_{1,\text{shared},j} n^{\log_2 LR_j} \right] > \text{NRE}_j.

Proof.

Let \Delta denote the change in fleet cost when subsystem u_j is added to the shared catalog.

Without catalog inclusion, the cost for subsystem u_j across all classes is:

C_{\text{without}} = \sum_{i=1}^{k} N_i c_{\text{custom},j}.

With catalog inclusion, the production cost becomes:

C_{\text{with}} = \sum_{i=1}^{k} \sum_{n=1}^{N_i} c_{1,\text{shared},j} n^{\log_2 LR_j},

plus the one-time NRE cost:

C_{\text{NRE}} = \text{NRE}_j.

The net cost change is:

\Delta = C_{\text{with}} + C_{\text{NRE}} - C_{\text{without}}.

= \sum_{i=1}^{k} \sum_{n=1}^{N_i} c_{1,\text{shared},j} n^{\log_2 LR_j} + \text{NRE}_j - \sum_{i=1}^{k} N_i c_{\text{custom},j}.

Inclusion is beneficial when \Delta < 0:

\sum_{i=1}^{k} \sum_{n=1}^{N_i} c_{1,\text{shared},j} n^{\log_2 LR_j} + \text{NRE}_j < \sum_{i=1}^{k} N_i c_{\text{custom},j}.

Rearranging:

\sum_{i=1}^{k} N_i c_{\text{custom},j} - \sum_{i=1}^{k} \sum_{n=1}^{N_i} c_{1,\text{shared},j} n^{\log_2 LR_j} > \text{NRE}_j.

\sum_{i=1}^{k} N_i \left[ c_{\text{custom},j} - \frac{1}{N_i}\sum_{n=1}^{N_i} c_{1,\text{shared},j} n^{\log_2 LR_j} \right] > \text{NRE}_j.

\square

B.4 Theorem 5.3: Three-Regime Standardization Optimum

Statement. Let P_i(\sigma_i) be a twice continuously differentiable operational penalty function with P_i''(\sigma_i) > 0 for all \sigma_i \in [0, \sigma_i^{\max}], and P_i'(0) = 0. Let:

J_i(N_i, \sigma_i) = C_i(N_i, \sigma_i) + \lambda N_i P_i(\sigma_i).

Define:

A_i = \sum_{n=1}^{N_i} c_{1,\text{shared}} n^{\log_2 LR} - N_i c_{\text{custom}}.

Then:

\sigma_i^* =
\begin{cases}
0, & \text{if } J_i'(0) \ge 0,\\
(0, \sigma_i^{\max}), & \text{if } J_i'(0) < 0 < J_i'(\sigma_i^{\max}),\\
\sigma_i^{\max}, & \text{if } J_i'(\sigma_i^{\max}) \le 0.
\end{cases}

Proof.

The first derivative of J_i is:

J_i'(\sigma_i) = A_i + \lambda N_i P_i'(\sigma_i).

The second derivative is:

J_i''(\sigma_i) = \lambda N_i P_i''(\sigma_i).

Since P_i''(\sigma_i) > 0 and \lambda N_i > 0, we have J_i''(\sigma_i) > 0. Therefore J_i'(\sigma_i) is strictly increasing on [0, \sigma_i^{\max}].

Case 1: J_i'(0) \ge 0.

Since J_i' is strictly increasing, J_i'(\sigma_i) > 0 for all \sigma_i > 0. Therefore J_i is strictly increasing on (0, \sigma_i^{\max}], and the minimum occurs at \sigma_i = 0.

Case 2: J_i'(0) < 0 < J_i'(\sigma_i^{\max}).

Since J_i' is continuous and strictly increasing, by the Intermediate Value Theorem there exists a unique \sigma_i^* \in (0, \sigma_i^{\max}) such that J_i'(\sigma_i^*) = 0. Since J_i''(\sigma_i^*) > 0, this is a strict local minimum. Because J_i' is strictly increasing, J_i'(\sigma_i) < 0 for \sigma_i < \sigma_i^* and J_i'(\sigma_i) > 0 for \sigma_i > \sigma_i^*, so \sigma_i^* is the global minimum on [0, \sigma_i^{\max}].

Case 3: J_i'(\sigma_i^{\max}) \le 0.

Since J_i' is strictly increasing, J_i'(\sigma_i) < 0 for all \sigma_i < \sigma_i^{\max}. Therefore J_i is strictly decreasing on [0, \sigma_i^{\max}), and the minimum occurs at \sigma_i = \sigma_i^{\max}.

\square

B.5 Proposition 8.1: Local Fixed-Point Contraction

Statement. Within each locally fixed active-set region, the operator \mathcal{T} is Lipschitz with estimated local contraction constant L \le 0.34 < 1.

Proof sketch.

The operator \mathcal{T} maps a mass vector \mathbf{m} to a corrected mass vector:

\mathcal{T}(\mathbf{m}) = \mathbf{m}_{\text{dry,base}} + \mathbf{m}_{\text{interface}}(N^*(\mathbf{m})) + \mathbf{m}_{\text{internal}}(\boldsymbol{\sigma}^*(\mathbf{m})).

Within a fixed active-set region, the functions N^*(\mathbf{m}) and \boldsymbol{\sigma}^*(\mathbf{m}) are smooth because the active constraints (selected module classes, catalog membership, door mechanism) do not change. Therefore \mathcal{T} is differentiable in this region.

The Jacobian of \mathcal{T} is:

\nabla \mathcal{T} = \frac{\partial \mathbf{m}_{\text{interface}}}{\partial N^*} \frac{\partial N^*}{\partial \mathbf{m}} + \frac{\partial \mathbf{m}_{\text{internal}}}{\partial \boldsymbol{\sigma}^*} \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{\sigma}^*}{\partial \mathbf{m}}.

The sensitivity \frac{\partial N^*}{\partial \mathbf{m}} derives from Thesis I's optimality conditions. At the converged solution, the first-order condition for N^* implies that \frac{\partial U}{\partial N} = 0, where U is the carrier-level utility. By the implicit function theorem:

\frac{\partial N^*}{\partial \mathbf{m}} = -\left( \frac{\partial^2 U}{\partial N^2} \right)^{-1} \frac{\partial^2 U}{\partial N \partial \mathbf{m}}.

Similarly, \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{\sigma}^*}{\partial \mathbf{m}} derives from the standardization optimization. Under the boundary-optimum regime (Theorem 5.1), \boldsymbol{\sigma}^* = \boldsymbol{\sigma}^{\max}, which is independent of \mathbf{m}, so this term vanishes. Under the interior-optimum regime (Theorem 5.3), the derivative is:

\frac{\partial \sigma_i^*}{\partial \mathbf{m}} = -\left( \lambda N_i P_i''(\sigma_i^*) \right)^{-1} \frac{\partial A_i}{\partial \mathbf{m}}.

Numerical estimation at the converged solution yields:

\Vert \nabla \mathcal{T}(\mathbf{m}^*) \Vert_{\infty} \le 0.34.

By the Mean Value Theorem, for any two points \mathbf{m}_1, \mathbf{m}_2 in a convex neighborhood of \mathbf{m}^* within the active-set region:

\Vert \mathcal{T}(\mathbf{m}_1) - \mathcal{T}(\mathbf{m}_2) \Vert_{\infty} \le \sup_{\mathbf{m} \in [\mathbf{m}_1, \mathbf{m}_2]} \Vert \nabla \mathcal{T}(\mathbf{m}) \Vert_{\infty} \cdot \Vert \mathbf{m}_1 - \mathbf{m}_2 \Vert_{\infty}.

If the neighborhood is sufficiently small that \Vert \nabla \mathcal{T} \Vert_{\infty} \le L < 1 throughout, then \mathcal{T} is a contraction on this neighborhood.

By the Banach fixed-point theorem, the iteration \mathbf{m}^{(k+1)} = \mathcal{T}(\mathbf{m}^{(k)}) converges to the unique fixed point \mathbf{m}^* within this neighborhood.

Handling of discrete variables. At active-set boundaries, \mathcal{T} may be discontinuous due to changes in N^* (integer) or catalog membership. These transitions are handled separately by checking whether the fixed point remains interior to the region. If not, the active set is updated, and iteration resumes in the new region. The global convergence is therefore piecewise, with each piece governed by a local contraction.

