Appendix D: Fixed-Point Iteration Convergence Details

D.1 Operator Definition

The fixed-point operator \mathcal{T} maps a mass vector \mathbf{m} \in \mathbb{R}^{10} to a corrected mass vector:

\mathcal{T}(\mathbf{m}) = \mathbf{m}_{\text{dry,base}} + \mathbf{m}_{\text{interface}}(N^*(\mathbf{m})) + \mathbf{m}_{\text{internal}}(\boldsymbol{\sigma}^*(\mathbf{m})),

where:

· \mathbf{m}_{\text{dry,base}} = (15000, 12000, 300, 40, 60, 35, 80, 50, 1000, 80)^T kg is the baseline dry mass vector for classes (H, L, C, S, E, T, R, P, A_c, A_p).
· \mathbf{m}_{\text{interface}}(N^*) is the interface mass as a function of optimal module count N^*.
· \mathbf{m}_{\text{internal}}(\boldsymbol{\sigma}^*) is the internal interconnect mass as a function of optimal standardization vector \boldsymbol{\sigma}^*.

D.2 Sensitivity of N^* to \mathbf{m}

From Thesis I, the carrier-level optimization maximizes utility U(\mathcal{A}) over architecture space. The first-order condition at the optimum is:

\frac{\partial U}{\partial N} = 0.

By the implicit function theorem:

\frac{\partial N^*}{\partial \mathbf{m}} = -\left( \frac{\partial^2 U}{\partial N^2} \right)^{-1} \frac{\partial^2 U}{\partial N \partial \mathbf{m}}.

The second derivative \frac{\partial^2 U}{\partial N^2} is negative at the interior optimum (strict concavity in N), ensuring the inverse exists.

D.3 Sensitivity of \boldsymbol{\sigma}^* to \mathbf{m}

Under the boundary-optimum regime, \boldsymbol{\sigma}^* = \boldsymbol{\sigma}^{\max}, which is independent of \mathbf{m}, so:

\frac{\partial \boldsymbol{\sigma}^*}{\partial \mathbf{m}} = 0.

Under the interior-optimum regime (Theorem 5.3), the derivative is:

\frac{\partial \sigma_i^*}{\partial \mathbf{m}} = -\left( \lambda N_i P_i''(\sigma_i^*) \right)^{-1} \frac{\partial A_i}{\partial \mathbf{m}},

where:

A_i = \sum_{n=1}^{N_i} c_{1,\text{shared}} n^{\log_2 LR} - N_i c_{\text{custom}}.

D.4 Jacobian Computation

The Jacobian of \mathcal{T} at the converged solution is computed via finite differences:

[\nabla \mathcal{T}]_{ij} \approx \frac{\mathcal{T}_i(\mathbf{m} + h \mathbf{e}_j) - \mathcal{T}_i(\mathbf{m})}{h},

with h = 10^{-3} \Vert \mathbf{m} \Vert_{\infty}. The step size is chosen to remain within the active-set region.

The computed Jacobian at the converged solution is:

\nabla \mathcal{T} \approx
\begin{pmatrix}
0.02 & 0.01 & 0.03 & 0.05 & 0.04 & 0.03 & 0.05 & 0.04 & 0.02 & 0.03 \\
0.01 & 0.02 & 0.02 & 0.04 & 0.03 & 0.02 & 0.04 & 0.03 & 0.01 & 0.02 \\
\vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \vdots
\end{pmatrix}.

The infinity norm is:

\Vert \nabla \mathcal{T} \Vert_{\infty} = \max_i \sum_j |[\nabla \mathcal{T}]_{ij}| \approx 0.34.

D.5 Convergence Trace

The iteration proceeds as follows:

Iteration k N^* \Vert \mathbf{m}^{(k+1)} - \mathbf{m}^{(k)} \Vert_{\infty} (kg)
0 188 —
1 174 800
2 171 120
3 170 15
4 170 2
5 170 <1

The iteration converges to \mathbf{m}^* = (15800, 12600, 320, 44, 66, 38, 88, 55, 1050, 86)^T kg, with N^* = 170.

D.6 Error Analysis

The truncation error from finite-difference gradient estimation is O(h) \approx 10^{-3}. The local contraction constant L = 0.34 implies that each iteration reduces the error by a factor of at least 0.34. After five iterations, the error is reduced by 0.34^5 \approx 4.5 \times 10^{-3}, which is consistent with the observed convergence from 800 kg to less than 1 kg.

The Lipschitz estimate is local to the converged solution. It is not a global bound. Active-set transitions could introduce larger changes, but the converged solution is interior to its active-set region, so no transitions occur near convergence.

