Appendix E: Parametric Family Criteria Formalization

E.1 Definition

A parametric family \mathcal{F}_j for subsystem u_j is a set of component designs sharing a common architecture. Two designs \mathbf{c}_{i,j} and \mathbf{c}_{i',j} belong to the same parametric family if and only if all of the following hold:

E.1.1 Architecture Code Identity

Both designs share the same subsystem architecture code. Examples:

· Solar arrays: "deployable rigid-panel, multi-junction GaAs cells, single-axis gimbal"
· Batteries: "prismatic Li-ion cells, passive thermal control"
· Thrusters: "cold-gas, heated, single-valve"
· Reaction wheels: "fixed-speed wheel, magnetic bearings"

E.1.2 Parameter Range

All key performance parameters lie within \pm 50\% of the family baseline:

\forall \theta \in \Theta_j, \quad 0.5 \cdot \theta_{\text{baseline}} \le \theta \le 1.5 \cdot \theta_{\text{baseline}}.

Examples of key parameters:

· Solar array: area, power output, deployed length
· Battery: capacity, voltage
· Thruster: thrust, I_{sp}, propellant mass
· Reaction wheel: momentum capacity, torque

E.1.3 Interface Identity

Mechanical and electrical interfaces to the module bus are identical:

· Same connector type and pinout
· Same mounting pattern and bolt circle
· Same power input voltage range
· Same data bus protocol

E.1.4 Qualification Status

Qualification status and dominant failure modes are unchanged:

· Same TRL
· Same qualification test program
· Same critical failure modes and mitigation strategies

E.2 Anti-Inflation Lemma

Lemma E.1. Under the four criteria above, the parametric family extension cannot inflate \sigma_i by grouping substantively different designs.

Proof. Suppose two designs \mathbf{c}_1 and \mathbf{c}_2 are grouped in the same parametric family \mathcal{F}_j. By criterion 2, all key parameters are within \pm 50\% of baseline. By criterion 1, the architecture is identical. By criterion 3, interfaces are identical. By criterion 4, qualification is identical. Therefore the designs are substantively similar: they are variants of the same design, not different architectures. Grouping them is therefore legitimate commonality, not inflation. \square

E.3 Application to Catalog

In the OMU catalog, the parametric extension applies to:

· Solar arrays: modular panel segments of varying area within a single architecture family.
· Batteries: varying capacity within a cell form factor.
· Truss nodes: varying length within a structural standard.
· Radiators: varying area within a panel family.
· Robotic arms: varying reach within an actuator family.
· Water tanks: varying volume within a pressure vessel family.

The extension does not apply to:

· Avionics: high-performance custom computers are not parametric variants.
· ECLSS core: crew-critical life support is mission-specific.
· Pressure shells: core habitat shells are parametric within a family, but the interior outfitting is not.

