Appendix F: Monte Carlo Simulation Methodology

F.1 Purpose

The Monte Carlo simulation propagates uncertainty in empirical parameters to uncertainty in the derived quantities N^* and CW-DCI.

F.2 Parameter Distributions

F.2.1 Standardization Ceilings

Peripheral classes:

\sigma_{\text{periph}}^{\max} \sim \text{Beta}(\alpha_p, \beta_p) = \text{Beta}(12, 2).

The Beta distribution parameters are chosen to match empirical estimates:

\mu = \frac{\alpha}{\alpha + \beta} = \frac{12}{14} = 0.857,

\sigma^2 = \frac{\alpha\beta}{(\alpha+\beta)^2(\alpha+\beta+1)} = \frac{24}{196 \times 15} = 0.00816,

\sigma = 0.0903.

Core classes:

\sigma_{\text{core}}^{\max} \sim \text{Beta}(\alpha_c, \beta_c) = \text{Beta}(3, 9).

\mu = \frac{3}{12} = 0.25,

\sigma^2 = \frac{27}{144 \times 13} = 0.01442,

\sigma = 0.1201.

F.2.2 Learning Rates

Learning rates are sampled uniformly within published ranges:

LR_{\text{avionics}} \sim \mathcal{U}(0.80, 0.85),

LR_{\text{power}} \sim \mathcal{U}(0.82, 0.88),

LR_{\text{propulsion}} \sim \mathcal{U}(0.85, 0.90),

LR_{\text{structures}} \sim \mathcal{U}(0.92, 0.97),

LR_{\text{pressure}} \sim \mathcal{U}(0.95, 0.98).

F.2.3 Internal Interconnect Mass Fractions

Peripheral modules:

f_{\text{internal, periph}} \sim \mathcal{N}(0.05, 0.02^2) \text{ truncated to } [0.03, 0.08].

Core modules:

f_{\text{internal, core}} \sim \mathcal{N}(0.045, 0.015^2) \text{ truncated to } [0.03, 0.06].

F.3 Simulation Procedure

For each Monte Carlo iteration s = 1, \ldots, N_{\text{sim}}:

1. Sample \sigma_i^{\max} for all ten classes from their Beta distributions.
2. Sample learning rates LR_j for all subsystems from their uniform distributions.
3. Sample internal interconnect mass fractions from their truncated normal distributions.
4. Compute the corrected mass vector:
   m_i^{(s)} = m_{\text{dry,base},i} \cdot (1 + f_{\text{internal},i}^{(s)}).
5. Run the fixed-point iteration with maximum 5 iterations to obtain N^{*(s)} and \boldsymbol{\sigma}^{*(s)}.
6. Compute CW-DCI^{(s)} using the sampled \sigma_i^{\max} and the population vector.
7. Record N^{*(s)} and CW-DCI^{(s)}.

F.4 Output Statistics

The simulation outputs probability density functions for N^* and CW-DCI. Reported statistics include:

· Mean and standard deviation of N^*
· 5th and 95th percentiles of CW-DCI
· Median CW-DCI
· Correlation between input parameters and outputs (sensitivity analysis)

F.5 Computational Details

The simulation is implemented in Python using NumPy for random sampling and SciPy for statistical distributions. No machine-learning libraries or trained models are used. The fixed-point iteration is deterministic given the sampled parameters; the only randomness is in the parameter sampling.

For N_{\text{sim}} = 10{,}000, the computation completes in approximately 30 seconds on a standard laptop. The results are stable to increasing N_{\text{sim}}, indicating convergence of the Monte Carlo estimates.

